{"id":1880,"date":"2020-12-09T16:48:18","date_gmt":"2020-12-10T00:48:18","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1880"},"modified":"2024-09-01T15:48:48","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T22:48:48","slug":"codrone-python-functions","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-functions\/","title":{"rendered":"Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c2e45\" name=\"6a221586c2e45\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a function?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code into one named set. For example, to connect to the last CoDrone you paired with, you could write multiple lines of code to do all of this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">Have I ever paired with CoDrone?\r\nIf so, begin Bluetooth search\r\nIdentify CoDrone addresses\r\nDoes the address match with what I last paired with?\r\nIf so, send the confirm signal\r\nTest the connection \r\nSend ready signal\r\nIf not, keep trying\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Or you could just use the function <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.pair()<\/code>at the beginning of every new program![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c2fc8\" name=\"6a221586c2fc8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Defining and calling functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The first step to writing your own function is the term <strong>def<\/strong>. You are telling Python that you are \u201cdefining a function\u201d.<\/p>\n<p>Next comes your function name followed by parentheses and a colon. Your function name can be multiple words, however, you need to add an underscore between words. For example, to create a function that greets a user, your function could look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def greet_user():<\/pre>\n<p>Add a new indented line for the body of the function and a comment that explains the function\u2019s purpose.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def greet_user():\r\n    \"\"\"Display a simple greeting\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    print(\"Hello!\")\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n\r\ngreet_user()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>The triple quotation marks are known as a <strong>docstring <\/strong>and explain the function\u2019s purpose.<\/p>\n<p>Try running this code now. What happened?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c303e\" name=\"6a221586c303e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Parameters in functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Aher every function are parentheses, and this is where the parameters go. Parameters are variables the function needs to use, but not all functions will have parameters! In the previous example, you might have noticed that the function had an empty set of parentheses.<\/p>\n<p>Think of a function as the processes part of a large factory. Some processes can run as is. For example, the function in the last step printed &#8220;Hello!&#8221; every time it was called. However, some processes need more information to run, like a program that will greet you personally by name!<\/p>\n<p>Let\u2019s add the parameter username into the parentheses in the same example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def greet_user(username): #username is a parameter\r\n    \"\"\"Display a simple greeting\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    print(\"Hello,\" + username.title() + \"!\")\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n<\/pre>\n<p><strong>Tip:<\/strong>\u00a0adding the \u201c .title()\u201d capitalizes the first letter of each word!<\/p>\n<p>Run this example code and notice what the console reads. Notice what\u2019s different between this string and the previous string?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c30a6\" name=\"6a221586c30a6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Arguments<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This next part may confuse you so read this carefully. When you call the function and write something to become \u201cusername\u201d, that new something is called an <strong>argument<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Here&#8217;s an example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">def greet_user(username): #username is a parameter\r\n    \"\"\"Display a simple greeting\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    print(\"Hello, \" + username.title() + \"!\")\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n\r\ngreet_user('kelly') # kelly is the argument\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>The name \u201cKelly\u201d is the argument. Kelly was passed from the function \u201cgreet_user()\u201d to being stored inside the parameter, \u201cusername\u201d. Think of it like Kelly is entering the front door to a candy store. Behind the front door is the actual store that shows the candy being made. She passed from the initial opening into where it will be used.<\/p>\n<p>So the console will print this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">Hello, Kelly!<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c30fc\" name=\"6a221586c30fc\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make your own function with one parameter. Next, test it by printing the results!<\/p>\n<p>Use this template if you need help.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def insert_function_name_here(insert parameter here):\r\n    \"\"\" This is a comment that describes what the function does\"\"\"\r\n    insert function statements here\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c3153\" name=\"6a221586c3153\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Multiple parameter functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Adding another parameter to a function takes a comma and a name for the new parameter.<\/p>\n<p>Like so:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def describe_pet(animal_type, pet_name):\r\n    '''Display pet information'''\r\n\r\n    print(\"I have a \" + animal_type + \".\")\r\n    print(\"My \" + animal_type + \"'s name is \" + pet_name + \".\")\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>The comma separates the parameters. Be careful! The order of the parameters is the order that they\u2019ll be printed in.<\/p>\n<p>Use the example code above and run it with your own arguments. Don\u2019t have a pet? Pretend you have one (maybe a flamingo?) and use it with this function.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c31c7\" name=\"6a221586c31c7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Square function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make your own function with two parameters: power and time. These two will determine how big your square will be!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def square(power, time):\r\n    # Input square code that uses a for loop\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Remember that to make a square you need to execute a pitch forward and turn. What parameter goes into the set_pitch command? Power or time? Don&#8217;t forget to set the directions back to 0 after you move.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.set_pitch(power)\r\ndrone.move(time)\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\ndrone.set_yaw(power)\r\ndrone.move(time)\r\ndrone.set_yaw(0)<\/pre>\n<p>The power and time will be set when the function is called. For example, if you want the power to be set at 30 and the time for each command to be 1 second, then you would call your function in your main program like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">square(30, 1)<\/pre>\n<p>Finish the square function to loop the code four times. Try calling the function with various parameter values until you find the right combination of power and time to make a square!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Hint:\u00a0<\/strong>See the example below if you need some help!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def square(power, time):\r\n\"\"\" This function makes a square.\"\"\"\r\n    for i in range(0,4):\r\n        drone.set_pitch(power)\r\n        drone.move(time)\r\n        drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n        drone.set_yaw(power)\r\n        drone.move(time)\r\n        drone.set_yaw(0)\r\n\"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c3226\" name=\"6a221586c3226\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]A return function is slightly different from the functions you just saw. A return function returns a value or set of values. The values that are returned are called <strong>return values<\/strong>. Very creative, we know.<\/p>\n<p>Here&#8217;s an example code to follow along:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def get_formatted_name(first_name, last_name):\r\n    \"\"\" Return a neatly formatted full name\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    full_name = first_name + \" \" + last_name\r\n    return full_name\r\n\r\n    \"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n\r\nsuperhero = get_formatted_name('Iron', 'Man')\r\nprint(superhero)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>The function is called get_formatted_name(). It has two parameters: first_name and last_name. The function prints a third variable called full_name with a \u201c.title()\u201d, allowing it to capitalize the first letter of each word.<\/p>\n<p>Then, the function is called so it can be used for a fourth variable named superhero. This fourth variable is not part of the function, which you can tell by its lack of indentation. The superhero&#8217;s name is added using both of the function&#8217;s parameters.<\/p>\n<p>Run the code to see which superhero shows up, and then try changing the names to add superheroes of your own![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c3278\" name=\"6a221586c3278\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make your own function with at least two parameters and a return statement.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Tip:\u00a0<\/strong>Use the template below if you need help.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def insert_function_name_here(insert parameter here , insert parameter here): \"\"\" This is a comment that describes what the  function does\"\"\"\tinsert function statements here\r\n    return some variable\t\"\"\" End of function \"\"\"\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221586c32c0\" name=\"6a221586c32c0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make 4 functions and each one should reflect the different types of functions you used today. One function can have one parameter; another can have two parameters; a third can have three or more parameters with a return statement, and the last one could be three or more parameters with a return statement and using an if and else statement.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 What is a function? [vc_column_text]Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code into one named set. For example, to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-functions\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1759,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":808,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,144,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2164,2131,2123,2156,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-1880","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1880","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":3,"href":"https:\/\/archi